1- User opens their browser, enters "www.foobar.com" into their search box
2- Browser asks DNS to switch it to an IP address
3- DNS responds with 8.8.8.8 (this time 8.8.8.8 is a virtual IP shared by 2 load balancers in a cluster)
4- After Browser gets it, it sends an HTTPS request to that address (on port 443, remember cause its not secured)
5- The request hits the active load balancer (HAProxy #1 or #2, depending on which one is currently active in the cluster)
6- The load balancer terminates SSL (uses the SSL certificate for www.foobar.com to decrypt HTTPS into plain HTTP inside the private network)
7- The load balancer looks at its config and picks one of the web servers (for example using Round Robin: first request to Web Server 1, second to Web Server 2, third back to Web Server 1, etc..)
8- The load balancer forwards the HTTP request to the chosen web server
9- On that web server, Nginx receives the request first
10- Nginx checks: if it’s a normal static file (files stored as-is on disk, ex: HTML, .png, .jpg, .css, .js), Nginx sends it directly back to the load balancer
11- If it’s a non-static/dynamic request, Nginx forwards it to one of the application servers (these are on separate machines that only run app code)
12- The application server receives the request, runs the backend code, and if it needs data, it talks to the database server (a dedicated MySQL server)
13- MySQL on the DB server handles the queries (reads/writes). Optionally there can still be a Primary DB and Replica DB, but now they live on dedicated DB machines instead of mixed web/app/DB servers
14- The application server builds the HTTP response and sends it back to the web server (Nginx)
15- Nginx sends the response back to the load balancer (over HTTP inside the private network)
16- The load balancer sends the response back to the user over the existing HTTPS connection
17- If the active load balancer fails, the second load balancer in the cluster takes over the virtual IP 8.8.8.8 automatically, so users can still reach the site

For every additional element, why am I adding it
Extra load balancer (HAProxy) configured as cluster with the other one:
- To avoid having a single point of failure at the load balancer level
- If one HAProxy instance dies, the other one takes over the virtual IP and continues serving traffic
- Increases availability and reliability of the entry point to the whole thing
Split web server on its own server(s):
- Web servers (Nginx) now focus only on handling HTTP, static files, and proxying to app servers
- Easier to scale just the web layer (add more Nginx servers if static traffic is high)
- Keeps web concerns separate from app and DB, which makes troubleshooting cleaner
Split application server on its own server(s):
- Application servers now only run the backend code and business logic
- We can scale the app layer independently (if app logic is heavy, add more app servers without touching DB or web servers)
- Separates CPU-heavy app logic from Nginx and from the database, improving performance and isolation
Split database on its own server(s):
- Database now lives on dedicated DB server(s), optimized only for storage and queries
- Easier to scale DB storage
- Keeps data more secure and isolated; if application or web crashes it don’t directly mess with DB processes
Extra server:
- Needed to host one of the split roles or to host the second HAProxy in the cluster
- Overall goal is to move away from "everything on the same box" and build separate tiers that can scale and fail independently
